selector_to_html = {"a[href=\"#submodules\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Submodules<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#submodules\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2>", "a[href=\"annular/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.ducts.annular<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.ducts.annular\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>Annular ducts about the machine\u2019s shaft.</p><p><a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"annular/AnnularDuct.html#pyskyfire.ducts.annular.AnnularDuct\" title=\"pyskyfire.ducts.annular.AnnularDuct\"><code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">AnnularDuct</span></code></a> is a passage between a hub and a shroud, both walls of\nrevolution about the shaft (+x). Its walls are curves in the meridional\n<code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">(x,</span> <span class=\"pre\">r)</span></code> plane, so it can turn between axial and radial: a turbine\u2019s inlet\nor exhaust annulus, or the crossover that carries a pump stage\u2019s radial\ndischarge round to the next eye. It is built on\n<code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">MeridionalPassage</span></code>, with its span lines drawn normal\nto the mean line so the area they sweep is the flow area.</p>", "a[href=\"../regen/index.html#module-pyskyfire.regen\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.regen<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.regen\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><h2>Submodules<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#submodules\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2>", "a[href=\"volute_section/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.ducts.volute_section<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.ducts.volute_section\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>Volute cross-section families.</p><p>A volute cross-section is a closed planar region measured in a section plane\nthat contains the machine axis. Its two coordinates are</p>", "a[href=\"pipe/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.ducts.pipe<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.ducts.pipe\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>Explicit pipe geometry construction and pressure-loss marching.</p><p>A <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"pipe/Pipe.html#pyskyfire.ducts.pipe.Pipe\" title=\"pyskyfire.ducts.pipe.Pipe\"><code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Pipe</span></code></a> is a duct swept along a <code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Path</span></code>: a circular bore,\noptionally with a concentric core inside it, such as a centrebody or the inner\nline of coaxial tubing. It contains the same <code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">PathStation</span></code> and\nconnection objects that its solver uses. <code class=\"xref py py-func docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">generate_path_stations()</span></code> is\nonly a convenience for turning human-friendly corner vertices into tangent\nstations and connections; users may construct the resolved objects directly.\nWhere the section varies, the pipe adds the stations its loss march needs by\nitself; see <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"pipe/Pipe.html#pyskyfire.ducts.pipe.Pipe\" title=\"pyskyfire.ducts.pipe.Pipe\"><code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Pipe</span></code></a>.</p>", "a[href=\"half_moon/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.ducts.half_moon<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.ducts.half_moon\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>The half-moon manifold section: a chamber wall on one side, an ellipse on the other.</p><p>A manifold wrapped around a thrust chamber is not a free shape. One side of it\nis the chamber itself \u2013 whatever the wall happens to do at that station \u2013 and\nthe other is a dome the designer picks. So the section is an arbitrary inner\ncurve, a half-ellipse outboard of it, and a fillet at each end blending the two.\nReal hardware of this kind reads as a D at the cylindrical part of a chamber and\nas a kidney where the wall curves.</p>", "a[href=\"area_law/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.ducts.area_law<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.ducts.area_law\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>Rules that set how a volute cross-section grows with azimuth.</p><p>An area law answers one question: at polar angle <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">theta</span></code> measured from the\ntongue, how big should the passage be? It answers it as a residual rather than\nas an area, because the shaped laws constrain the area <em>and</em> the centroid\nradius together, and both follow from the section size. The volute solver\ndrives that residual to zero.</p>", "a[href=\"port/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.ducts.port<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.ducts.port\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>How a collecting passage hands its flow to a pipe.</p><p>A volute and a chamber manifold both end at a flange, and both need a duct to\nget there, but they leave in different directions. A volute\u2019s flow is already\ntravelling round the passage, so its duct leaves tangentially and turns; a\nmanifold has no such direction, so its duct runs straight out of the dome its\nsection is built on, aimed wherever the packaging wants it. Those are two ports,\nnot two classes of machine, and either passage may carry either one \u2013 a\nmanifold fed tangentially is a real arrangement, and it should not cost a second\nimplementation of the same duct.</p>", "a[href=\"volute/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.ducts.volute<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.ducts.volute\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>Volute geometry generation and one-dimensional loss analysis.</p><p>A <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"volute/Volute.html#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute\" title=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute\"><code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Volute</span></code></a> resolves its own geometry on construction: it sweeps the\npolar angle from the tongue, and at each station solves the area law for the\ncross-section size that satisfies it. <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"volute/Volute.html#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute.solve\" title=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute.solve\"><code class=\"xref py py-meth docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Volute.solve()</span></code></a> then runs Aungier\u2019s\nmeanline loss analysis over the resolved geometry.</p>", "a[href=\"#module-pyskyfire.ducts\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.ducts<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.ducts\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>Duct hydraulics shared by cooling channels, piping and manifolds.</p><p>This subpackage contains shared path geometry, explicit pipe hydraulics,\nannular ducts, orifices and orifice plates, and volute and collector manifolds. It depends only on <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"../common/index.html#module-pyskyfire.common\" title=\"pyskyfire.common\"><code class=\"xref py py-mod docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.common</span></code></a> for\nplanar section primitives and friction correlations and <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"../thermo/index.html#module-pyskyfire.thermo\" title=\"pyskyfire.thermo\"><code class=\"xref py py-mod docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.thermo</span></code></a> for fluid state records,\nand it does not import from <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"../regen/index.html#module-pyskyfire.regen\" title=\"pyskyfire.regen\"><code class=\"xref py py-mod docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.regen</span></code></a> or <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"../network/index.html#module-pyskyfire.network\" title=\"pyskyfire.network\"><code class=\"xref py py-mod docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.network</span></code></a>,\nso cooling channels, turbopump hardware and engine-network adapters can all\ndepend on it without a cycle.</p>", "a[href=\"manifold/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.ducts.manifold<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.ducts.manifold\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>A ring that shares flow out to a circumference, or gathers it back in.</p><p>Where a volute collects swirling flow and is sized to conserve the angular\nmomentum it takes in, a manifold has no swirl to conserve. Fluid arrives\nthrough a port and has to travel round to reach the far channels, or comes in\nall round and has to travel to the port; either way the circumferential velocity\nis a cost rather than a design variable. So a manifold is not a volute with a\ndifferent area law, and it is not named the way one is.</p>", "a[href=\"orifice/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.ducts.orifice<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.ducts.orifice\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>Orifices: short restrictions described by a flow area and a discharge coefficient.</p><p>An <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"orifice/Orifice.html#pyskyfire.ducts.orifice.Orifice\" title=\"pyskyfire.ducts.orifice.Orifice\"><code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Orifice</span></code></a> is a hole that discharges into a large volume: an injector\norifice into the chamber, a valve seat into the line behind it, a bleed into a\ncavity. Its jet\u2019s kinetic energy is not recovered, so the whole drop from the\ninlet to the discharge pressure is lost. An <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"orifice/OrificePlate.html#pyskyfire.ducts.orifice.OrificePlate\" title=\"pyskyfire.ducts.orifice.OrificePlate\"><code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">OrificePlate</span></code></a> is a thin\nround hole sitting in a pipe: the flow approaches it at the pipe velocity, and\npart of the drop recovers downstream as the jet re-expands to the pipe bore.\nThe plate follows ISO 5167; see its notes.</p>", "a[href=\"path/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.ducts.path<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.ducts.path\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>Shared centerline construction, rigid placement, and transported frames.</p><p>Paths contain geometry only. Flow areas and fluid states belong to components.\nAll vectors use Cartesian coordinates and all angles are radians.</p>", "a[href=\"exit_cone/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.ducts.exit_cone<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.ducts.exit_cone\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>The duct carrying collected flow from a volute to its pipe flange.</p><p>A volute delivers into piping whose diameter is usually fixed by a standard,\nand the direction it delivers in varies with how the machine is packaged: some\noutlets run straight on from the full-collection plane, some curve round until\nthey point radially outward, and real hardware sits anywhere between. The exit\ncone is the transition that reconciles the passage with that flange.</p>", "a[href=\"manifold_law/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.ducts.manifold_law<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.ducts.manifold_law\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>Rules that set how a manifold\u2019s passage grows as it gathers flow.</p><p>A volute\u2019s area law answers to swirl: the passage is sized so the angular\nmomentum it collects is conserved, and departing from that is what costs the\ntangential loss. A chamber manifold has no swirl to conserve. Its circumferential\nvelocity is a nuisance rather than a design variable \u2013 it exists only because\nfluid has to travel round to reach the far channels \u2013 so the laws here answer a\ndifferent question: how much dynamic head does the passage carry, and is it the\nsame everywhere the channels tap into it?</p>", "a[href=\"../thermo/index.html#module-pyskyfire.thermo\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.thermo<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.thermo\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>Fluid properties, thermodynamic states and combustion-gas aerothermodynamics.</p><p>CEA-backed APIs are loaded on first access so fluid and nozzle-loss utilities\ncan be imported without loading CEA, including through the package root.</p>", "a[href=\"../network/index.html#module-pyskyfire.network\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.network<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.network\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><h2>Submodules<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#submodules\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2>", "a[href=\"../common/index.html#module-pyskyfire.common\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.common<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.common\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><h2>Submodules<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#submodules\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2>"}
skip_classes = ["headerlink", "sd-stretched-link"]

window.onload = function () {
    for (const [select, tip_html] of Object.entries(selector_to_html)) {
        const links = document.querySelectorAll(` ${select}`);
        for (const link of links) {
            if (skip_classes.some(c => link.classList.contains(c))) {
                continue;
            }

            tippy(link, {
                content: tip_html,
                allowHTML: true,
                arrow: true,
                placement: 'auto-start', maxWidth: 600, interactive: false,
                onShow(instance) {MathJax.typesetPromise([instance.popper]).then(() => {});},
            });
        };
    };
    console.log("tippy tips loaded!");
};
